<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marwan II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/750">750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marwan II was the last Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, reigning from 744 until his death in 750. He came to power during civil wars and spent his reign fighting internal revolts. He was defeated by the Abbasid revolutionaries at the Battle of the Zab in 750 and killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> in the east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marwan-ii.jpg" alt="Marwan II" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 8-th century mint masters · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_of_Marwan_II_ibn_Muhammad,_AH_127-132.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marwan ibn Muhammad belonged to the Marwanid house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>. He was the son of Muhammad ibn Marwan, a grandson of the caliph Marwan I and a nephew of the caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-marwan">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan</a>. Historical tradition places his birth around 695. Political opponents used his mother's lineage against him during the civil wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-fitna">Third Fitna</a>: hostile accounts claimed she was a non-Arab concubine of Kurdish origin who was already pregnant when Muhammad acquired her.</p><p>In 732-733 Caliph Hisham appointed Marwan governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. In 735-736 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, devastated it, took three fortresses of the Alans and made peace with Tumanshah; further raids in 739-740 brought tribute. For several years he supervised campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> on the northwestern frontiers. When a plot against al-Walid II took shape, he wrote from Armenia urging his relatives to preserve the unity of the Umayyad house, but he was ignored, and Yazid III deposed al-Walid. Yazid then made him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, where he lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Marwan at first opposed Yazid III and then gave him allegiance. When Yazid died early, Marwan ignored the successor Yazid had named, Ibrahim ibn al-Walid, and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. Ibrahim received assurances of personal safety and accompanied him to Hisham's residence at Rusafah. Marwan named his sons Ubaydallah and Abdullah as heirs. Anti-Umayyad feeling was strong, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and his reign was almost entirely devoted to holding the empire together.</p><p>He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a> (Homs) after a ten-month siege and fought a Kharijite rebellion led by al-Dahhak ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> al-Shaybani, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>; Marwan besieged the rebels at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> for six months before driving them out. In Khurasan the governor Nasr ibn Sayyar faced rivals while Abbasid envoys arrived, and during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> of 747 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> unfurled the standards of their revolt. Nasr died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Rayy</a> on 9 November 748. Marwan's campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 749 against the Bashmuric Revolt failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Marwan suffered a decisive defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-abbas-al-saffah">Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zab">Great Zab</a>, at the Battle of the Zab, where over 300 members of the Umayyad family died. He fled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, Jordan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he was caught and killed on 6 August 750. His sons escaped to what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, and Abdallah died fighting there.</p><p>His death ended Umayyad fortunes in the East and was followed by a mass killing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. Almost the entire dynasty was killed, except the prince Abd al-Rahman ibn Mu'awiya, who escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and founded an Umayyad dynasty there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marwan II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Marwan ibn Muhammad</b>'s great campaign of 737 forced a temporary submission, but they held the line of the Caucasus against Islamic expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 750 the last Umayyad caliph, <b>Marwan II</b>, was defeated at the Battle of the Great Zab, pursued into Egypt and killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Arab armies first took Tbilisi in the 640s, and around 736 the Umayyad general <b>Marwan ibn Muhammad</b> made it the seat of an emir governing Georgian lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-tbilisi">Emirate of Tbilisi</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Marwan II</b> sent Abd al-Malik ibn Atiyya with Syrian troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/ibadi-imamate-of-hadramawt">Ibadi Imamate of Hadramawt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bilad al-Sham. Died in Abusir, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.9,31.2&amp;year=740" aria-label="Abusir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5361 1072 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Marwan II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 1342h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 1342h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=29.9,31.2&amp;year=740">What was here in 740 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
